S_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_PURPOSES#] [#IABV2_LABEL_FEATURES#] [#IABV2_BODY_FEATURES_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_FEATURES#] [#IABV2_LABEL_PARTNERS#] [#IABV2_BODY_PARTNERS_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_PARTNERS#] About Cookies are small text files that can be used by websites to make a user's experience more efficient. The law states that we can store cookies on your device if they are strictly necessary for the operation of this site. For all other types of cookies we need your permission. This website uses different types of cookies. Some cookies are placed by third party services that appear on our pages. You can at any time change or withdraw your consent from the cookie declaration on our website. Please state your consent ID and date when you contact us regarding your consent. For more information about the use of cookies at TU/e you can visit: https://www.tue.nl/en/storage/disclaimer Do not sell or share my personal information Deny Allow selection Customize Allow all Skip to content Menu Close the menu Menu Eindhoven University of Technology Education Research Our university Working at TU/e News and Events Impact × search Search Nederlands NL Search Working at TU/e Vacancies Scientific Staff Scientific Staff Academic Perspectives Irène Curie Program Learning & Development Personal Grants Partner Career Support Program Scientific Staff Vacancies Support Staff Support Staff Learning & Development Support Staff Vacancies PhD EngD At TU/e Why TU/e Conditions of Employment Application Process Support for Internationals Home Working at TU/e Vacancies PhD in Energy-Effici... Working at TU/e Directly to Create job alert Login to (re)view your application(s) Login as an employee to view internal vacancies PhD in Energy-Efficient Sensors and Computing Hardware from 2D Materials Personal type: Scientific staff Field of expertise: PhD Organisation: Department of Electrical Engineering Apply no later than: 19-11-2026 Full-time equivalent: 1.0 FTE Salary: € 3.204 - € 4.051 Apply now Introduction Are you eager to shape the next generation of chips using atomically thin materials? In this PhD position, you will develop 2D nanomaterials and devices for more energy-efficient sensing and computing. The project will be undertaken within the NanoComputing Research Lab in the Department of Electrical Engineering at Eindhoven University of Technology (TU/e), at the heart of Europe’s leading semiconductor region. Job Description In this PhD position, you will investigate 2D materials as a platform for next-generation sensing and computing devices. You will develop growth recipes for 2D materials and fabricate and characterize devices that could contribute to emerging energy efficient brain-inspired computing hardware. What you'll contribute to Specifically, you will: develop metal-organic chemical vapour deposition (MOCVD) growth recipes for large-area 2D materials, such as MoS₂ and WSe₂, with controlled defect densities; optimize transfer processes to integrate the grown 2D materials onto target device substrates, enabling heterogeneous integration; develop scalable fabrication processes in the TU/e cleanroom (NanoLab@TU/e) for device arrays, including sensors and transistors; engineer metal–semiconductor and dielectric interfaces to improve device performance; characterize materials and devices, and work with circuit designers to link material properties to device and circuit performance; publish your results in leading journals and present them at international conferences; supervise Bachelor’s and Master’s student projects. Your research environment You will join the NanoComputing Research Lab ( NanoComputing Research Lab ), in the Integrated Circuits group of the Department of Electrical Engineering at TU/e. You will be supervised by Dr. Abhishek Singh Dahiya (project leader) and Prof. Aida Todri-Sanial. You will have access to TU/e’s state-of-the-art cleanroom and characterization facilities. What we're looking for A master’s degree in electrical engineering, nanotechnology, or a related field, with a solid foundation in semiconductor materials and device physics, and prior experience in the growth of nanomaterials (preferably 2D materials using CVD), nanofabrication processes such as atomic layer deposition, lithography, etching etc., and 2D material transfer process. Job Requirements A master’s degree (or an equivalent university degree) in electrical engineering, nanotechnology, or a related field. A research-oriented attitude. Ability to work in an interdisciplinary team. Motivated to develop your teaching skills and mentor bachelor/master’s students. Fluent in spoken and written English (C1 level). Conditions of Employment A meaningful job in a dynamic and ambitious university, in an interdisciplinary setting and within an international network. You will work on a beautiful, green campus within walking distance of the central train station. In addition, we offer you: Full-time employment for four years, with an intermediate assessment after nine months. You will spend a minimum of 10% of your four-year employment on teaching tasks, with a maximum of 15% per year of your employment. Salary and benefits (such as a pension scheme, paid pregnancy and maternity leave, partially paid parental leave) in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities , scale P (min. € 3,204 - max. € 4,051 gross base salary per month (full-time)). In addition to your base salary, you will receive an 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus, both calculated based on your annual gross base salary. Generous leave options: a standard 29 days (based on a 38 hour working week) per year that you can increase to 41 days by working two hours more per week (flexible working hours). This is prorated if you work part-time. As a TU/e employee, you participate in the ABP pension scheme, providing retirement pension and pension benefits for surviving dependents and occupational disability. TU/e pays 70% of the pension premium, while employees c"
    },
