PURPOSES_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_PURPOSES#] [#IABV2_LABEL_FEATURES#] [#IABV2_BODY_FEATURES_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_FEATURES#] [#IABV2_LABEL_PARTNERS#] [#IABV2_BODY_PARTNERS_INTRO#] [#IABV2_BODY_PARTNERS#] About Cookies are small text files that can be used by websites to make a user's experience more efficient. The law states that we can store cookies on your device if they are strictly necessary for the operation of this site. For all other types of cookies we need your permission. This website uses different types of cookies. Some cookies are placed by third party services that appear on our pages. You can at any time change or withdraw your consent from the cookie declaration on our website. Please state your consent ID and date when you contact us regarding your consent. For more information about the use of cookies at TU/e you can visit: https://www.tue.nl/en/storage/disclaimer Do not sell or share my personal information Deny Allow selection Customize Allow all Skip to content Menu Close the menu Menu Eindhoven University of Technology Education Research Our university Working at TU/e News and Events Impact × search Search Nederlands NL Search Working at TU/e Vacancies Scientific Staff Scientific Staff Academic Perspectives Irène Curie Program Learning & Development Personal Grants Partner Career Support Program Scientific Staff Vacancies Support Staff Support Staff Learning & Development Support Staff Vacancies PhD EngD At TU/e Why TU/e Conditions of Employment Application Process Support for Internationals Home Working at TU/e Vacancies PhD in supramolecula... Working at TU/e Directly to Create job alert Login to (re)view your application(s) Login as an employee to view internal vacancies PhD in supramolecular interactions in organic mixed ionic-electronic conductors Personal type: Scientific staff Field of expertise: PhD Organisation: Department of Chemical Engineering and Chemistry Apply no later than: 22-11-2026 Full-time equivalent: 1.0 FTE Salary: € 3.204 - € 4.051 Apply now Introduction Are you fascinated by how molecular-scale interactions determine the real-world performance of functional materials? In this PhD project, you will study a new class of materials that find application in future technologies ranging from neuromorphic computing and bioelectronics to energy storage. Are you ready to help unravel the inner workings of these fascinating materials by investigating them during operation? Job Description Your research In your PhD project, you will uncover how intermolecular interactions and supramolecular morphology govern the functionality of organic mixed ionic-electronic conductors (OMIECs). Bridging the fields of OMIECs and supramolecular chemistry, you will investigate these relationships through in-depth operando characterization. OMIECs are soft materials that conduct both electrons and ions, highly promising for applications ranging from energy storage and solar fuels to bioelectronics and neuromorphic computing. However, electrons, ions, solvent and the molecular structure all reorganize during operation, making it difficult to disentangle how molecular-scale interactions ultimately determine macroscopic function. You will develop characterization methods and experimental setups to probe intermolecular interactions as OMIECs operate. Spectroelectrochemistry will be a key tool; developing operando cells, setups, and experiments that enable you to discern dynamic spectral signatures of molecular packing. By following changes in supramolecular organization during electrochemical doping, your work will reveal how doping affects supramolecular organization. You will also determine how supramolecular packing governs doping efficiency and dynamics, linking morphology to device-relevant properties, by studying varied samples of available supramolecular building blocks. These fundamental insights will provide a supramolecular knob for tuning material functionality, thereby introducing new ways to control material behavior. The key outcome of your research is a mechanistic framework describing the bidirectional interplay between OMIEC doping and supramolecular interactions. Building on this framework, you will ultimately design switchable supramolecular OMIEC systems in collaboration with other researchers, targeting applications in in-material computing. Beyond these systems, your fundamental insights will contribute to the general development of next-generation computing and adaptive bioelectronics. Your environment and support As a member of the Optoelectronic Mixed Conductors group at the Department of Chemical Engineering and Chemistry, you will conduct your research under the supervision of Prof. Tom van der Pol. Your project is highly interdisciplinary, with opportunities to collaborate with researchers across the department. You will become part of a broad and vibrant scientific community, including the Institute for Complex Molecular Systems. At TU/e and within the Optoelectronic Mixed Conductors group, curiosity-driven research and personal development go hand in hand. You will have the freedom to pursue ambitious scientific questions while receiving the guidance and support needed to develop your own ideas and expertise. Your supervisory team will support you in developing the skills, confidence, and scientific perspective needed to uncover the mechanisms that govern OMIECs and to shape your own path as a researcher. Job Requirements We are looking for an enthusiastic self-motivated researcher who holds an MSc degree in chemistry, physics, material science, or equivalent . Experience with functional material characterization is essential, and spectroscopy or electrochemistry is preferred. Prior experience with organic semiconductors, supramolecular chemistry, or operando characterization is highly valued. You possess strong analytical and problem-solving skills and enjoy hands-on experimental research. You are curious, creative, and eager to develop new experiments, setups, knowledge,"
    },
